WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:05.940
Experimental questions become much easier when you understand what the

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:06.960
instrument is actually measuring.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.500
Vernier calipers have outer jaws for external dimensions, inner jaws for

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:15.640
internal dimensions, and a depth rod for the depth of a vessel.

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:21.200
Choose the right contact surfaces, align the instrument, and use gentle

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:21.640
contact

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:23.380
rather than squeezing the object.

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:27.480
Before measuring, close the clean jaws and check the zero.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:32.160
For a direct vernier with ten vernier divisions matching nine main scale

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.940
divisions, one vernier division is zero point nine millimeters when a main

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:39.100
scale division is one millimeter.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:42.640
Their difference, zero point one millimeters, is the least count.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.720
Read the main scale mark immediately to the left of the vernier zero.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:50.940
Then find the vernier mark that coincides with a main scale mark.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.180
Add that coincidence number times the least count.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.780
Here is our original example: the main scale reading is two point three

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:04.010
centimeters, the seventh vernier mark coincides, and least count is zero point

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:05.660
zero one centimeters.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:09.040
The raw reading is two point three seven centimeters.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.380
If the closed jaws show positive zero error of zero point zero three

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:14.360
centimeters, subtract it.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:18.700
The corrected result is two point three four centimeters.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:22.080
Pause and check the sign: zero correction is negative zero error.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:27.000
Repeat readings at different positions to detect uneven diameter, and avoid

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:28.540
parallax when judging alignment.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.280
Least count tells you resolution; it does not automatically guarantee the same

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:34.060
absolute accuracy.

00:01:34.416 --> 00:01:38.116
A screw gauge converts rotation into a small linear movement.

00:01:39.076 --> 00:01:43.316
Pitch is the distance the spindle advances in one full revolution, not the

00:01:43.316 --> 00:01:44.256
total length of the screw.

00:01:45.096 --> 00:01:48.916
If pitch is zero point five millimeters and the circular scale has fifty

00:01:48.916 --> 00:01:52.216
divisions, least count is zero point zero one millimeters.

00:01:53.096 --> 00:01:56.796
Take the visible sleeve reading, including an exposed half millimeter mark

00:01:56.796 --> 00:01:56.996
when

00:01:56.996 --> 00:02:00.836
appropriate, then add the circular scale reading times least count.

00:02:01.326 --> 00:02:05.516
In our example, the sleeve shows one point five millimeters and the circular

00:02:05.516 --> 00:02:06.456
reading is twenty eight.

00:02:07.416 --> 00:02:10.791
Add zero point two eight millimeters to obtain a raw reading of one point

00:02:10.791 --> 00:02:11.023
seven

00:02:11.023 --> 00:02:11.776
eight millimeters.

00:02:12.936 --> 00:02:16.076
Suppose zero error is negative zero point zero two millimeters.

00:02:17.116 --> 00:02:21.109
Corrected reading is raw reading minus that negative error, giving one point

00:02:21.109 --> 00:02:22.076
eight zero millimeters.

00:02:23.036 --> 00:02:26.116
Keep the trailing zero because it communicates the stated resolution.

00:02:27.156 --> 00:02:31.636
A ratchet helps apply consistent light contact instead of crushing a wire or

00:02:31.636 --> 00:02:31.876
sheet.

00:02:32.956 --> 00:02:36.616
Backlash is slack between the screw and nut that can make reversing rotation

00:02:36.616 --> 00:02:37.136
misleading.

00:02:38.196 --> 00:02:41.756
Approach the final contact in the same rotation direction, following the

00:02:41.756 --> 00:02:42.536
instrument's procedure.

00:02:43.356 --> 00:02:47.986
If you overshoot, back off sufficiently and approach again; do not treat a

00:02:47.986 --> 00:02:48.236
tiny

00:02:48.236 --> 00:02:50.716
reversal as guaranteed real spindle movement.

00:02:51.736 --> 00:02:55.356
Check zero before and after a set of measurements, and measure a wire at

00:02:55.356 --> 00:02:57.036
different locations and orientations.

00:02:57.776 --> 00:03:02.256
One last trap: if a question gives diameter but a later equation requires

00:03:02.256 --> 00:03:04.816
radius, divide by two before squaring.

00:03:05.616 --> 00:03:08.556
The instrument reading and the derived quantity are separate steps.

00:03:08.832 --> 00:03:11.452
Now a metre scale becomes a balance.

00:03:12.292 --> 00:03:16.092
First find where the empty scale balances horizontally on a narrow support.

00:03:16.832 --> 00:03:21.312
That point identifies its center of gravity; do not assume every real scale

00:03:21.312 --> 00:03:23.172
balances exactly at fifty centimeters.

00:03:24.492 --> 00:03:27.692
Suspend a known mass on one side and an unknown mass on the other.

00:03:28.312 --> 00:03:32.252
If the pivot is at the scale's center of gravity, the scale's own weight has

00:03:32.252 --> 00:03:33.532
zero moment about the pivot.

00:03:34.312 --> 00:03:37.952
For equilibrium, the clockwise and anticlockwise moments balance.

00:03:38.732 --> 00:03:43.952
Both gravitational forces contain g, which cancels, leaving known mass times

00:03:43.952 --> 00:03:47.892
its perpendicular distance equal to unknown mass times its perpendicular

00:03:47.892 --> 00:03:48.352
distance.

00:03:49.412 --> 00:03:52.332
Our example scale balances at the fifty centimeter mark.

00:03:53.472 --> 00:03:57.330
A one hundred gram mass hangs at twenty centimeters, so its lever arm is

00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:57.652
thirty

00:03:57.652 --> 00:03:58.012
centimeters.

00:03:58.892 --> 00:04:02.552
The unknown hangs at seventy centimeters, so its lever arm is twenty

00:04:02.552 --> 00:04:03.012
centimeters.

00:04:04.212 --> 00:04:08.119
Unknown mass is one hundred times thirty divided by twenty, or one hundred

00:04:08.119 --> 00:04:08.752
fifty grams.

00:04:10.792 --> 00:04:15.192
Pause before using the marks themselves: twenty and seventy are positions, not

00:04:15.192 --> 00:04:15.712
lever arms.

00:04:16.892 --> 00:04:20.572
Keep the scale horizontal, account for hanger masses where relevant, and avoid

00:04:20.572 --> 00:04:22.912
a broad support that lets the pivot position wander.

00:04:23.652 --> 00:04:27.612
If the pivot is not at the empty scale's center of gravity, its weight adds

00:04:27.612 --> 00:04:29.252
another torque and must be included.

00:04:30.792 --> 00:04:34.432
The support force passes through the pivot, so it contributes no torque about

00:04:34.432 --> 00:04:34.912
that point.

00:04:35.772 --> 00:04:40.212
This experiment is an application of rotational equilibrium, not a rule that

00:04:40.212 --> 00:04:42.432
all forces on the scale vanish individually.

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:45.540
A pendulum gradually swings through smaller angles.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:50.400
Instead of merely watching it slow down, we can use amplitude to estimate how

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:51.940
its mechanical energy changes.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:56.600
For small angles, with displacement amplitude A measured approximately

00:04:56.600 --> 00:05:00.979
horizontally and effective length L to the bob's center, the restoring

00:05:00.979 --> 00:05:01.480
constant

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:03.780
is m g divided by L.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:08.560
At a turning point the bob's speed is zero, and its mechanical energy is

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:11.400
approximately one half of that constant times A squared.

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:16.300
Keep length and bob mass fixed, and energy is proportional to amplitude

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:16.620
squared.

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:21.660
Record the maximum displacement on the same side after known numbers of

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:23.220
oscillations, or at measured times.

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:27.780
Square those amplitudes and plot A squared vertically against time

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:28.300
horizontally.

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:32.480
A decreasing curve shows mechanical energy being transferred to the

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:35.680
surroundings through air resistance and friction at the support.

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:40.300
Energy is not destroyed; mechanical energy of the pendulum is what decreases.

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:44.540
Suppose amplitude falls from eight centimeters to four centimeters.

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:49.060
The amplitude ratio is one half, but the energy ratio is one quarter.

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:53.000
Seventy five percent of the initial mechanical energy has been dissipated.

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:55.800
Take a moment and separate those percentages.

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:00.300
Weak damping with a simple linear resistance model gives approximately

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:04.920
exponential amplitude decay, but do not force every measured A squared graph

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:05.880
into a straight line.

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:10.500
Avoid drafts, release gently without a push, and use the small angle

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:11.180
approximation.

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:16.300
Reading moving turning points has uncertainty, so repeated observations and

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:18.660
clear reference marks matter as much as the equation.

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:22.920
Let's measure the speed of sound using a tuning fork and a water filled

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:23.680
resonance tube.

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:27.960
The water surface forms the closed end of the adjustable air column.

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:32.920
Hold the vibrating fork near the open end without touching the tube, and vary

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:35.420
the water level slowly to find strong resonance.

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:40.500
In the displacement picture, the closed end is a node and the open end is

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:41.740
approximately an antinode.

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:46.820
The effective open boundary lies slightly beyond the tube mouth, producing an

00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:47.740
end correction e.

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:52.740
The first resonant length satisfies L one plus e equal to one quarter

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:53.200
wavelength.

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:57.460
The next satisfies L two plus e equal to three quarters wavelength.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:02.480
Subtracting cancels e, so wavelength is twice the difference between the two

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:03.060
measured lengths.

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:06.800
Multiply by the known fork frequency to get sound speed.

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:12.080
Suppose frequency is five hundred hertz, first length sixteen centimeters, and

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:14.040
second length fifty centimeters.

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:19.120
Their difference is thirty four centimeters, or zero point three four meters.

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:22.460
Wavelength is zero point six eight meters, and speed is three hundred forty

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:23.280
meters per second.

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:27.430
The same data give end correction of one centimeter, since a quarter

00:07:27.430 --> 00:07:27.920
wavelength

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:30.160
is seventeen centimeters.

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:33.473
Pause here: changing the tube length did not change the driving fork

00:07:33.473 --> 00:07:33.960
frequency.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:38.440
These are two resonant lengths for the same frequency, not a claim that the

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:39.880
fork suddenly plays its third harmonic.

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:44.420
Repeat resonance positions while raising and lowering the water, record

00:07:44.420 --> 00:07:47.760
temperature, and distinguish the displacement pattern from the opposite

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:48.600
pressure node pattern.
